Caden DeNike wrote this.

The work is his: the scheduling method, the design of the calendar, the
reader that takes one back off a picture, and the program around them. His
copyright notice appears at the top of every source file and in the LICENSE.

Under the GNU General Public License you are welcome to use this, change it
and pass it on. What you may not do is present it as your own: section 5 of
the licence requires that a modified version carry prominent notices saying
you changed it and when, and that it keep the notices already in it. Removing
them is not a style choice -- it terminates the rights the licence grants,
under section 8.

Releases are signed, and so are the commits and tags behind them from the
point signing was set up. A signature proves who published a copy rather than
who wrote it, but a history signed by somebody else did not come from here.
